================================================================================
Where the facts come from
================================================================================

Every rule this tool depends on was checked against a primary source. Where a
community source disagreed with the game, the game won.

  The selection rule for both formats. A legacy overrides list is scanned in
  order and the last entry whose every predicate satisfies `value >= threshold`
  wins; a range_dispatch selects the entry with the largest threshold that is
  `<= value`, and falls back when none is. The second is stated in the item
  model definition documentation on minecraft.wiki and is observable in every
  vanilla definition that uses it.

  Which modern property each legacy predicate corresponds to, and the units.
  Taken from the vanilla 26.3 definition of the item that used the predicate, as
  published by misode/mcmeta (https://github.com/misode/mcmeta, assets branch),
  compared against the 1.21.3 model file it replaced (the 1.21.3-assets tag of
  the same repository). Those two files are the same migration this tool
  performs, done by the people who wrote the format:

    bow.json           using_item, then use_duration with scale 0.05 and the
                       legacy thresholds 0.65 and 0.9 unchanged
    crossbow.json      charge_type select with cases arrow and rocket, whose
                       fallback is using_item over crossbow/pull with the legacy
                       thresholds 0.58 and 1.0 unchanged
    compass.json       has_component on minecraft:lodestone_tracker, with the
                       same entries under target lodestone and target spawn
    recovery_compass.json   one dispatch with target recovery
    clock.json         context_dimension select, source daytime in the
                       overworld and source random outside it
    brush.json         use_cycle with period 10 and scale 0.1, keeping the
                       legacy thresholds 0.25, 0.5 and 0.75
    light.json         block_state select on the level property, cases 0..15,
                       with the level-15 model as the fallback
    beehive.json       block_state select on honey_level
    elytra.json        condition on minecraft:broken
    diamond_chestplate.json and the other trimmed armour
                       trim_material select naming each material

  The trim material indexes (quartz 0.1 through amethyst 1.0) are the
  item_model_index values of the vanilla trim_material registry as it stood in
  1.21.3, which is what the legacy trim_type predicate returned.

  minecraft:bundle/fullness returning the weight of the bundle_contents
  component, minecraft:use_cycle returning the remaining use ticks modulo
  period, and range_dispatch's scale being applied to the property value before
  thresholds are compared, come from the item model definition documentation on
  minecraft.wiki (https://minecraft.wiki/w/Items_model_definition). No vanilla
  definition uses bundle/fullness, so there is no file to check it against.

  That a legacy integer CustomModelData arrives in floats[0] of the modern
  component, which is why the emitted dispatch needs no index field, is the
  behaviour of the game's own data fixer for that component.

  The pack.mcmeta rules — the format-64 boundary between the supported_formats
  era and the min_format/max_format era, both new fields being required
  together, supported_formats being rejected above 64, and pack_format needing
  to be at least 15 in a multi-version pack — come from the pack metadata codec
  in the client, via a publicly available decompilation used as read-only
  evidence. No decompiled code is reproduced here.

  The version-to-format table in PackFormats.kt lists releases only and was
  derived from the version summary published by misode/mcmeta, which mirrors
  Mojang's version manifest. The numbers are facts about the game.

================================================================================
Prior art
================================================================================

None of these projects' code is used.

  misode/mcmeta                 no licence file — treated as read-only reference
                                https://github.com/misode/mcmeta
                                The vanilla assets of every version as plain
                                directories. The reason this tool could be
                                checked against Mojang's own migration of each
                                item instead of against guesswork.

  Misode's generators           https://misode.github.io
                                Browser generators for data and asset files,
                                including item model definitions. The right tool
                                for writing one definition by hand; this one is
                                for a pack that already has three hundred.

  ComunidadAylas/PackSquash     AGPL-3.0      https://github.com/ComunidadAylas/PackSquash
                                Pack optimisation, done properly. Run it after
                                this, as a separate program.

  unnamed/creative              MIT           https://github.com/unnamed/creative
                                A resource pack object model and serialiser for
                                the JVM. The right dependency if you are doing
                                this inside a plugin.

  Oraxen, Nexo, ItemsAdder      Various, some closed or source-available with
                                restrictions. Each generates packs of the kind
                                this tool reads and each has its own migration
                                for its own configuration files. No code from any
                                of them is present, and none of their config
                                formats is read or written.

